/// \defgroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories Die, damage and collide modules and their factory functions
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Sixteen of BFME 2's die, damage and collide module classes,
///        with 13 of their registered instance factories and 11 of their
///        INI data factories (each class page links the ones documented
///        here).
///
/// These are event modules. A die module reacts when its object dies, a
/// damage module when the object is damaged, and a collide module when the
/// object touches another (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle, step 4).
/// Object INI lists each of them like any other behavior module
/// (`Behavior = RefundDie ModuleTag_...`). This group documents the code
/// that creates the modules and their data, the INI fields each data class
/// accepts, and each class's role; it does not describe every behavior in
/// full. BFME 2 registers many other die, damage and collide modules (Zero
/// Hour ones such as CreateObjectDie, FXListDie and SquishCollide among
/// them); only the classes listed below are covered here. Not every
/// factory of these classes is documented either: the instance factories
/// of KeepObjectDie and RebuildHoleExposeDie, the data factories of
/// UpgradeDie and CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof, and the shared data
/// factories that BoneFXDamage, HeroDie and HordeMemberCollide are
/// registered with (see below) are left out, and CrateCollide registers no
/// instance factory at all.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`.
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes the flow step by
/// step; it applies here unchanged:
/// - The **data factory** (\c friend_newModuleData) runs while INI loads.
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the data class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** (\c friend_newModuleInstance) runs when an
///   object is built. ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object
///   and the module data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// Neither factory is called directly: the module factory reaches them only
/// through the registered pointers.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// Every module name registered for a class in this group is spelled
/// exactly like the class (no module is registered as \c CrateCollide;
/// see below), and every one is registered as a logic (behavior) module,
/// together with its interface mask. Read with Zero Hour's interface
/// bit values (`Common/Module.h`), the masks are:
/// - **Die only**: HeroDie, KeepObjectDie, RebuildHoleExposeDie, RefundDie,
///   UpgradeDie and CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof.
/// - **Die and damage**: DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie, which has an
///   \c onDamage step as well as its \c onDie step.
/// - **Damage only**: BoneFXDamage, CallHelpOnDamage,
///   HordeTransportContainDamage, ReflectDamage and EvacuateDamage.
/// - **Collide only**: CivilianSpawnCollide, HordeMemberCollide and the
///   crate collides that use CrateCollideModuleData (below).
/// - **Collide and update**: AODCrushCollide, which also has an \c update
///   step.
///
/// \par Shared data factories
/// The registration table does not pair every name with a data factory of
/// its own:
/// - \c HeroDie is registered with
///   SpecialPowerCompletionDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData, the data
///   factory of \c SpecialPowerCompletionDie, so both accept the same
///   fields. HeroDie has no data factory of its own in BFME 2.
/// - \c HordeMemberCollide is registered with the data factory that is
///   also registered under \c SquishCollide, so the two accept the same
///   fields.
/// - CrateCollideModuleData::friend_newModuleData is not registered under a
///   \c CrateCollide name. The table registers it as the data factory of
///   both \c HealCrateCollide and \c ShroudCrateCollide; the other crate
///   collides (MoneyCrateCollide, UnitCrateCollide, VeterancyCrateCollide
///   and SalvageCrateCollide) are registered with data factories of their
///   own.
/// - \c BoneFXDamage is registered with a generic data factory that other,
///   unrelated modules share; that factory is not documented here.
/// - The data factories of \c CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof and \c UpgradeDie
///   exist in BFME 2 but are not documented here; their class pages list
///   the fields their parse procedures add.
///
/// \par Fields every die module here accepts
/// The \c buildFieldParse procedures of the die module data classes in this
/// group first add a shared die table, then the class's own table (for
/// CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof, CreateObjectDie's procedure adds the
/// shared table). Read from the retail table, the shared fields are
/// \c DeathTypes, \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus, as in Zero Hour's
/// DieMuxData table, plus \c DamageAmountRequired (a real number) and
/// \c MinKillerAngle and \c MaxKillerAngle (angles). Zero Hour's table also
/// has \c VeterancyLevels, which the BFME 2 table does not. Whether a die
/// module tests these conditions is up to its \c onDie step; HeroDie's, for
/// example, does not (see its class page).
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - All 24 factories documented here have the same frame. Each allocates
///   with the global \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's
///   size and runs the class's constructor inside an exception frame that
///   frees the block if the constructor throws, as a C++ new-expression
///   requires. When the allocation returns null the constructor is skipped
///   and null is returned. A data factory does not check for that case
///   before parsing: with a reader it still hands the null pointer to
///   INI::initFromINIMultiProc.
/// - A data factory parses after construction and outside that frame, so
///   an INI error thrown while parsing does not free the new data.
/// - Each data factory passes its own class's \c buildFieldParse; the
///   class pages list the fields those procedures add, read from the
///   retail field tables.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has KeepObjectDie, RebuildHoleExposeDie, UpgradeDie,
/// SpecialPowerCompletionDie and BoneFXDamage, and registers HealCrateCollide
/// and ShroudCrateCollide on top of a shared CrateCollide base. It has none
/// of DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie, HeroDie, RefundDie,
/// CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof, CallHelpOnDamage,
/// HordeTransportContainDamage, ReflectDamage, EvacuateDamage,
/// AODCrushCollide, CivilianSpawnCollide or HordeMemberCollide.
/// Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources or factory and constructor units
/// for several of these (for example RefundDie, HeroDie, EvacuateDamage,
/// AODCrushCollide, CivilianSpawnCollide and HordeMemberCollide), so those
/// classes already exist in BFME 1.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their module tags and
///   the fields on the class pages. Object creation lists, FX lists,
///   upgrades, special powers, sciences and object filters that the fields
///   name are separate definitions.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, which data factory each name uses (so a
///   HeroDie block accepts exactly SpecialPowerCompletionDie's fields, and
///   a HordeMemberCollide block exactly SquishCollide's), the enumerations
///   behind the name lists (damage types, death types), and what each
///   module does with its fields. A new kind of reaction needs a new class
///   and a new ModuleFactory::init registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Multiplayer: these modules run in the game logic and their data comes
///   from \c Object blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Several fields (\c CollapsingTime, \c PostFilterTriggeredDuration,
/// \c TrackingTimeSpan and \c CallDelay) are parsed by
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which turns milliseconds into whole logic
/// frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details), while
/// RebuildHoleExposeDie's \c FadeInTimeSeconds is read as a plain real
/// number. A change to the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore
/// affects the two kinds of field differently.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die and damage module registered as
///        "DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie": runs an object creation list
///        when its object dies, but only after a matching kind of damage.
///
/// Read from the matched BFME 2 bodies:
/// - Its damage step records the current logic frame and the attacker's
///   player whenever a damage event carries the value named by
///   \c DamageTypeTriggersForDuration. Healing clears the recorded frame.
/// - Its die step first tests the shared die conditions (group page). If
///   the killing damage carries the value named by
///   \c DamageTypeTriggersInstantly, it runs \c CreationList from the
///   attacker at the dying object's position and destroys the object.
///   Otherwise, if a recorded trigger is younger than
///   \c PostFilterTriggeredDuration, it does the same with an object that
///   the recorded player supplies, subject to a further check on that
///   player that this page does not identify; once the window has passed
///   it forgets the player.
///
/// The recorded frame and player are the module's saved state.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (DamageFilteredCreateObjectDieModuleData), after the shared
/// die fields: \c CreationList (an object creation list),
/// \c DamageTypeTriggersInstantly, \c DamageTypeTriggersForDuration and
/// \c PostFilterTriggeredDuration (milliseconds, stored as logic frames).
/// Read from the retail table, both trigger fields are parsed by
/// INI::parseIndexList against a two-name list, \c NORMAL and
/// \c BECOME_UNDEAD, not against the damage-type names their field names
/// suggest. This page does not establish which damage value they are
/// compared with.
///
/// \see DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DamageFilteredCreateObjectDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DamageFilteredCreateObjectDieModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie: the shared die
///        fields plus the creation list and its damage triggers.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// DamageFilteredCreateObjectDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared
/// by every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie

/// \fn ModuleData *DamageFilteredCreateObjectDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie":
///        creates the module data for one module line and parses its block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while object INI loads. The constructor leaves the creation list empty
/// and the duration at zero. When \p ini is not null, the factory passes
/// DamageFilteredCreateObjectDieModuleData::buildFieldParse (the shared
/// die table, then the class's own table) to INI::initFromINIMultiProc.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block. When null,
///             parsing is skipped and the data keeps its defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see DamageFilteredCreateObjectDie


/// \class HeroDie
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die module registered as "HeroDie": when its object dies, it
///        resets the named special power on the controlling player's
///        objects (Zero Hour slot order: its ready frame).
///
/// Read from the matched BFME 2 body: the die step walks every object of
/// the dying object's controlling player and, on each one that has a
/// special power module for the module data's special power template,
/// calls the special power module's interface slot that Zero Hour's order
/// identifies as \c setReadyFrame, passing the current logic frame. If that
/// identity holds in BFME 2, the power becomes ready at once. The step does
/// not test the shared die conditions, so \c DeathTypes and the other
/// shared fields have no effect on it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// HeroDie has no data class of its own in BFME 2: the registration pairs
/// it with SpecialPowerCompletionDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData. Its
/// block therefore accepts the shared die fields plus
/// SpecialPowerCompletionDie's one field, \c SpecialPowerTemplate (a
/// special power name, resolved while the INI is read). Open-BFME-1's
/// \c game/ tree names a HeroDieModuleData class, with constructor and
/// field-parse units of its own, so BFME 1 appears to have given HeroDie
/// data of its own.
///
/// \see HeroDie::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *HeroDie::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HeroDie": creates the module for
///        one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The module data it
/// receives was built by
/// SpecialPowerCompletionDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData, the data
/// factory registered for both \c HeroDie and \c SpecialPowerCompletionDie.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HeroDie


/// \class KeepObjectDie
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die module registered as "KeepObjectDie"; in Zero Hour it lets a
///        dying object stay in the world as rubble.
///
/// Zero Hour: a die module for things that should leave rubble in the
/// world and have no other die module; without one, an object is removed
/// outright. Zero Hour's die step only tests the shared die conditions and
/// does nothing else, so what matters is that the module is present. BFME 2
/// adds two fields of its own (below); what the module does with them is
/// not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (KeepObjectDieModuleData), after the shared die fields:
/// \c CollapsingTime (milliseconds, stored as logic frames) and
/// \c StayOnRadar (boolean). Zero Hour's KeepObjectDie uses the plain die
/// module data and has neither. The constructor default for
/// \c CollapsingTime is a fixed count of logic frames, not milliseconds,
/// so its real-time length depends on the logic rate.
///
/// \see KeepObjectDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class KeepObjectDieModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for KeepObjectDie: the shared die fields plus the
///        collapse time and the radar flag.
///
/// Built once per module line by KeepObjectDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData
/// and shared by every object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *KeepObjectDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "KeepObjectDie": creates the module
///        data for one module line and parses its block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while object INI loads. The constructor runs the shared die module data
/// base constructor, then sets \c CollapsingTime to its frame-count
/// default and \c StayOnRadar to false. When \p ini is not null, the
/// factory passes KeepObjectDieModuleData::buildFieldParse (the shared die
/// table, then the class's own table) to INI::initFromINIMultiProc.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block. When null,
///             parsing is skipped and the data keeps its defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see KeepObjectDie


/// \class RebuildHoleExposeDie
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die module registered as "RebuildHoleExposeDie"; in Zero Hour a
///        dying structure leaves a rebuild hole that rebuilds it.
///
/// Zero Hour: when a structure with this module dies, and it belongs to an
/// active, non-neutral player and is not under construction, the module
/// creates the object named by \c HoleName at the structure's position and
/// angle, gives it the structure's geometry and script name, sets its
/// maximum health to \c HoleMaxHealth and starts the hole's rebuild of the
/// structure. With \c TransferAttackers, every AI that was attacking the
/// structure is told to transfer its attack to the hole. BFME 2 keeps the
/// module and adds the \c FadeInTimeSeconds field; its die step is not
/// described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (RebuildHoleExposeDieModuleData), after the shared die
/// fields: \c HoleName (an object template name, stored as text),
/// \c HoleMaxHealth (real), \c TransferAttackers (boolean, default true)
/// and \c FadeInTimeSeconds (real, in seconds, not converted to frames).
/// \c FadeInTimeSeconds is not in Zero Hour's table.
///
/// \see RebuildHoleExposeDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RebuildHoleExposeDieModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for RebuildHoleExposeDie: the shared die fields plus
///        the hole template, its health, the attacker transfer flag and the
///        fade-in time.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// RebuildHoleExposeDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *RebuildHoleExposeDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RebuildHoleExposeDie": creates the
///        module data for one module line and parses its block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while object INI loads. The constructor leaves the hole name empty,
/// the health and fade-in time at zero and \c TransferAttackers on. When
/// \p ini is not null, the factory passes
/// RebuildHoleExposeDieModuleData::buildFieldParse (the shared die table,
/// then the class's own table) to INI::initFromINIMultiProc.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block. When null,
///             parsing is skipped and the data keeps its defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see RebuildHoleExposeDie


/// \class RefundDie
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die module registered as "RefundDie": when its object dies, the
///        controlling player gets back a share of the object's cost.
///
/// Read from the matched BFME 2 body: the die step tests the shared die
/// conditions and skips objects with certain status bits set. It then
/// requires the controlling player to pass two checks, one against
/// \c UpgradeRequired when that is set and one against the
/// \c BuildingRequired object filter when that is set. The refund is the
/// object's recorded build cost times \c RefundPercent, rounded up to a
/// whole amount. A non-zero refund is added to the player's money, and the
/// \c GUI:AddCash string is shown as floating text just above the
/// object.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (RefundDieModuleData), after the shared die fields:
/// \c UpgradeRequired (an upgrade name), \c RefundPercent (a percentage,
/// default 0, so a block without it refunds nothing) and
/// \c BuildingRequired (an object filter). BFME 2's
/// INI::parseUpgradeTemplate looks \c UpgradeRequired up while the INI is
/// read and stores a null reference for an unknown name, without reporting
/// an error; the die step then skips the upgrade check, so a misspelled
/// name silently removes the requirement. The floating text comes from
/// the string table entry \c GUI:AddCash.
/// Because the refund changes a player's money, it is part of the game
/// logic and must be identical on every machine.
///
/// \see RefundDie::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      RefundDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class RefundDieModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for RefundDie: the shared die fields plus the
///        required upgrade, the refund share and the building filter.
///
/// Built once per module line by RefundDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData
/// and shared by every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *RefundDie::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "RefundDie": creates the module
///        for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see RefundDie

/// \fn ModuleData *RefundDieModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "RefundDie": creates the module data
///        for one module line and parses its block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while object INI loads. The constructor runs the shared die module data
/// base constructor, then clears the required upgrade, sets the refund
/// share to zero and constructs an empty building filter. When \p ini is
/// not null, the factory passes RefundDieModuleData::buildFieldParse (the
/// shared die table, then the class's own table) to
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block. When null,
///             parsing is skipped and the data keeps its defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see RefundDie


/// \class UpgradeDie
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die module registered as "UpgradeDie": when its object dies, the
///        upgrade its data names is removed from the controlling player or
///        from the object's producer.
///
/// Read from the matched BFME 2 body: the die step tests the shared die
/// conditions and looks up the upgrade named by \c UpgradeToRemove. A
/// player upgrade is removed from the dying object's controlling player.
/// Any other upgrade is removed from the object that produced the dying
/// object, if that producer still exists and has it.
///
/// Zero Hour: the module frees an upgrade in the producer, for objects
/// that an upgrade created (its example is a unit that builds drones).
/// Zero Hour's die step has only the producer case; the player-upgrade
/// case is new in BFME 2.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own field (UpgradeDieModuleData), after the shared die fields:
/// \c UpgradeToRemove, an upgrade name stored as text and looked up when
/// the object dies; a name that matches no upgrade does nothing.
///
/// \see UpgradeDie::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *UpgradeDie::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "UpgradeDie": creates the module
///        for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see UpgradeDie


/// \class CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Die module registered as "CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof": a
///        CreateObjectDie that fires only for the eldest object of its kind.
///
/// Read from the matched BFME 2 body: the module derives from
/// CreateObjectDie. Its die step runs an eldest-of-kind test over the
/// controlling player's objects and, only if the dying object passes,
/// continues with CreateObjectDie::onDie, which runs the creation list.
/// The test is helper code that this page does not describe further.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Its data's \c buildFieldParse adds CreateObjectDie's fields first (the
/// shared die fields, then \c CreationList, \c DebrisPortionOfSelf and
/// \c UpgradeRequired) and then one field of its own, \c ObjectFilter (an
/// object filter).
///
/// \see CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see CreateObjectDieIfEldestKindof


/// \class BoneFXDamage
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_die_damage_collide_module_factories
/// \brief Damage module registered as "BoneFXDamage": forwards body damage
///        state changes to the object's BoneFXUpdate.
///
/// Read from the matched BFME 2 bodies: when the object is created, the
/// module looks for a \c BoneFXUpdate module on the same object and throws
/// an INIException with the message "BoneFXDamage requires BoneFXUpdate"
/// if there is none. Whenever the body's damage state changes, it passes
/// the old and new states to BoneFXUpdate::changeBodyDamageState (Zero
/// Hour: which switches the object's bone effects to those of the new
/// state). Zero Hour has the same module; its creation check throws a
/// different exception.
///
/// \par Modding
/// BoneFXDamage has no fields of its own: it is registered with a generic
/// data factory that other modules share, which allocates only the base
/// module data and whose parse procedure adds an empty table. The effects
/// themselves are
/// configured on the \c BoneFXUpdate module, which must be on the same
/// object.
///
/// \see BoneFXDamage::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *BoneFXDamage::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "BoneFXDamage": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    shared with every other object of the template.
